The \textit{cuspidal end} of height $a$ is defined as the product $S^1 \times \left] a, + \infty \right[$, which is the quotient $\mathbb{Z} \backslash \left( \mathbb{R} \times \left] a, + \infty \right[ \right)$, endowed with the \textit{Poincaré metric}

\[ \begin{array}{lll}
        \mathrm{d}s^2_{\mathrm{hyp}} & = & \frac{\mathrm{d}x^2 + \mathrm{d}y^2}{y^2}
    \end{array} . \]

\noindent It is implicitly assumed here that $S^1$ is parametrized by $x \in \left[ 0, 1 \right[$.

\[ \begin{array}{lll}
                    \mathrm{d}s^2_{\mathrm{hyp}} & = & \frac{\mathrm{d}x^2 + \mathrm{d}y^2}{y^2}
                \end{array} . \]


The cuspidal end of height $a > 0$ is isometric to the punctured disk $D^{\times} \left( 0, \varepsilon \right)$ of radius $\varepsilon = \exp \left( - 2 \pi a \right)$ with the Poincar\'{e} metric

\[ \begin{array}{lll}
        \mathrm{d}s^2_{\mathrm{hyp}} & = & \frac{\left\vert \mathrm{d} z \right \vert^2}{\left( \left \vert z \right \vert \log \left \vert z \right\vert \right)^2}
    \end{array} . \]

\[ \begin{array}{lll}
                    \mathrm{d}s^2_{\mathrm{hyp}} & = & \frac{\left\vert \mathrm{d} z \right \vert^2}{\left( \left \vert z \right \vert \log \left \vert z \right\vert \right)^2}
                \end{array} . \]


